                <pb facs="0516" n="426" sameAs="frus:frus1934v03/pg426"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.5151/365: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-02-22T15:00:00+08:00">February 22, 1934—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 22—6:22 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>97. Your 43, February 17, 3 p.m.<anchor n="8" xml:id="d314-f1"/></p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>In well-informed circles, North China, it is not believed that the rise in
                        price of silver has been an important factor in the increase in
                        trade.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>It is believed that depreciation of United States dollar has been a very
                        important factor.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>Other factors have been (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>) increasing
                        confidence among businessmen in the United States, (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >b</hi>) purchases by American importers of Chinese products in
                        anticipation of further rise in price of silver.</item>
                    <label>4.</label>
                    <item>There is a belief among North China foreign bankers that a stabilization
                        of the price of silver would be a great benefit all around but they fear
                        that if price is stabilized too high there would result a sharp contraction
                        in trade and the Chinese Government might be inspired to depreciate Chinese
                        silver dollars.</item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
